{"reviewer_participant_id": "actor:dependence-c-reviewer-opus-09", "reviews": [{"case_id": "case:f5df454bf6b8da3d72c8", "verdict": "no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope", "bounded_statement": null, "root_cause": null, "issue_class": null, "evidence_atoms": [{"description": "The governing task explicitly withholds any identification semantics from the codes, so no repeated entry of a single independent source unit can be established from the visible files; the repetition of `k1` values is uninterpretable by instruction.", "observed_value": "Codes are declared opaque and non-identifying", "source_spans": [{"path": "task.md", "start_line": 1, "end_line": 1, "quoted_text": "Treat `k1` and `k2` as opaque codes; `tag` is an opaque annotation. This task does not establish whether either code identifies a source item within either collection."}]}, {"description": "The task authorizes application of the procedure to the complete staged row sequence, and the workflow does exactly that with no row subsetting or filtering, so the number of entries per row is exactly what was authorized.", "observed_value": "staged = rows (all 12 rows, no filtering)", "source_spans": [{"path": "task.md", "start_line": 1, "end_line": 1, "quoted_text": "Apply the named procedure to the complete staged row sequence."}, {"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 4, "end_line": 5, "quoted_text": "rows = list(csv.DictReader(Path(\"inputs/data.csv\").read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines()))\nstaged = rows"}]}, {"description": "The procedure applied is row-independent by construction (independent two-sample t-test on two disjoint collections), and the task states the row pairing carries no relationship, so the row-independent treatment matches the stated data semantics rather than violating them.", "observed_value": "st.ttest_ind(left, right); df=22.0 consistent with 12 vs 12 independent samples", "source_spans": [{"path": "task.md", "start_line": 1, "end_line": 1, "quoted_text": "The row pairing is a storage convention only; there is no matching, pairing, block, or other relationship between the items represented in that row."}, {"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 8, "end_line": 8, "quoted_text": "result = st.ttest_ind(left, right)"}, {"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 1, "end_line": 1, "quoted_text": "TtestResult(statistic=np.float64(-0.6793662204867575), pvalue=np.float64(0.5039915691282064), df=np.float64(22.0))"}]}, {"description": "Each measurement column is read once per row into its own sample vector, so no single table cell or row contributes more than one observation to either arm of the test.", "observed_value": "left = 12 values from column a; right = 12 values from column b", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 6, "end_line": 7, "quoted_text": "left = [float(row[\"a\"]) for row in staged]\nright = [float(row[\"b\"]) for row in staged]"}]}], "counterevidence_atoms": [{"description": "Strongest contrary signal: each `k1` value (u01–u06) occurs on exactly two rows, a pattern that in other contexts would indicate two measurements of one source unit entering an independence-assuming procedure twice (clustering / pseudoreplication).", "observed_value": "6 distinct k1 codes across 12 rows, each appearing twice", "source_spans": [{"path": "inputs/data.csv", "start_line": 2, "end_line": 5, "quoted_text": "u01,v07,t01,1.0,2.0\nu01,v11,t02,2.0,4.0\nu02,v06,t03,3.0,3.0\nu02,v09,t04,4.0,5.0"}, {"path": "inputs/data.csv", "start_line": 12, "end_line": 13, "quoted_text": "u06,v08,t11,11.0,13.0\nu06,v02,t12,12.0,12.0"}]}, {"description": "The workflow performs no grouping, aggregation, or cluster-robust adjustment on `k1`, and the reported df=22.0 reflects 24 observations treated as fully exchangeable — i.e., if the codes did identify units, the report would carry no correction for it.", "observed_value": "df=22.0, no grouping code present", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 6, "end_line": 8, "quoted_text": "left = [float(row[\"a\"]) for row in staged]\nright = [float(row[\"b\"]) for row in staged]\nresult = st.ttest_ind(left, right)"}]}], "falsification_attempt": "I tried to establish the issue rather than dismiss it. The candidate demonstration is that k1 takes only six distinct values across twelve rows, each exactly twice, so if k1 were a source-item identifier then every unit in the `a` collection would enter the independence-assuming t-test twice, inflating the effective n from 6 to 12 and understating the variance of the test statistic — a textbook repeated-unit entry into a row-independent procedure. To demonstrate this issue class I need the antecedent 'k1 identifies a source item' to be established by the visible files. It is not: the task states in terms that admit no reading otherwise that k1 is an opaque code and that the task 'does not establish whether either code identifies a source item within either collection.' Supplying that antecedent would require me to infer intent from the mere shape of the code values, which is precisely the unstated-intention guessing the review forbids. I also probed two alternative routes to the same issue class and both fail: (a) a row-level duplication route — no row or cell is read twice; each row contributes exactly one value to `left` and one to `right`, and df=22.0 confirms 12 and 12; (b) a cross-arm route, i.e. the same unit appearing in both samples — the task states the two collections are disjoint, so `a` and `b` items are not the same units, and k2 is unique per row in any case. I checked the inverse error too (that a paired procedure was owed): the task states the row pairing is storage convention with no relationship, so ttest_ind is the matching choice and the report is not a mis-specified paired analysis. The verdict therefore survives falsification: the repetition is visible but its interpretation as repeated independent-unit entry is expressly not established, and the row-level entry that did occur is exactly what the task authorized. Residual risk is confined to the possibility that the visible k1 pattern was intended as an identifier despite the disclaimer, which I decline to assume.", "cross_case_evidence_used": false, "unresolved_material_questions": [], "self_reported_confidence": "high"}]}